Raw dev notes -- The Unsung Heroes of Maintenance: Keeping the Lights On

Okay, so this past week or so has been less about flashy new features and more about the grind of keeping my various side projects actually *working*. It's a different kind of satisfaction, honestly, but a crucial one. It feels like I'm finally getting better at building things that don't just break the moment an external API sneezes.

**What are these things anyway?**
I've got a few personal scripts and tools running in the background. One is a little checker script that monitors something on a particular dating site (let's call it the "eharmony-checker" for simplicity, though it's not actually eharmony). Then there's a wrapper I built around a browser automation API, which I use for various small tasks. And finally, my RAG knowledge base pipeline, which is constantly trying to keep itself updated with new info. They're all pretty critical for different personal workflows.

**What went down this period?**
The biggest win was definitely making the eharmony-checker more robust. It was constantly hitting intermittent 401 Unauthorized errors, which meant it would just stop working until I manually re-logged it in. Super annoying. I finally implemented a proper retry mechanism that detects the 401, attempts a full re-login, and then retries the original request. It's a small change but a massive quality-of-life improvement. It's been running smoothly since.

On the browser automation front, I added some CI love. Now, when there's a new version of the underlying Python library for the automation API, my CI pipeline sends me an email via SMTP. I even put in a little test button for the email, which was surprisingly satisfying to click and see it land in my inbox. More importantly, I squashed a nasty hotfix bug in the `BrowserChatAPI` part of the wrapper. It was throwing 500 errors on follow-up queries, specifically in the 0.7.x branch. Turns out it was a subtle caching issue, needing `self._cache=core` to be explicitly set.

My RAG knowledge base pipeline was mostly just chugging along, doing its thing. The commits show it syncing, picking up a few comment updates, but no new posts this time around. It's good to see it consistently running and keeping the data fresh.

**The good, the bad, and the 500s:**
That 401 issue on the eharmony-checker was a real pain. Intermittent errors are the worst to debug because you can't reliably reproduce them. It took a while to figure out the exact sequence of events that led to the 401 and that a full re-login was the most reliable recovery. I initially tried just refreshing tokens, but that wasn't enough.

The 500 error in the browser automation wrapper was also a head-scratcher. "Follow-up queries" failing with a server error usually points to some state not being correctly maintained. The `self._cache=core` fix felt a bit like black magic, honestly, but it worked. It highlights how important it is to understand the underlying library's lifecycle and state management, especially when dealing with conversational APIs.

It's a constant battle against external APIs changing their minds or just having transient issues. Building resilience into these scripts feels like a never-ending task, but it's so rewarding when they just *work* without intervention.

**Personal reflections:**
There's a deep satisfaction in making something robust. It's not as flashy as building a new UI or implementing a complex algorithm, but it's the bedrock of reliable software. I'm learning that a significant part of "shipping" isn't just getting the first version out, but making sure it can withstand the real world. Debugging these kinds of issues, especially the intermittent ones, really hones my problem-solving skills. It teaches patience and methodical investigation. It also reinforces the value of good logging and monitoring, even for personal projects. It felt good to finally put those nagging issues to bed.

**What's next:**
Probably some more maintenance, or maybe a small feature for the RAG pipeline.
